Keep a field record that survives reinterpretation and transfer (@geo-field-record: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @geology, #source "../sources/Geological-Structures-Waldron-Snyder.lingua#r_2RC4KMK6ADFX90708NZH2F6RRW", #source "../sources/SGB-Geodiversity-Methods.lingua#r_7QW5KYAYK9SB2MT3R6MNCFAQWK", #source "../sources/GeMS-Map-Schema.lingua#r_6JP32YH79HZAV8JYYKVV1WER0J", #geology "../../../../hephaestus/inquiry/metrology/foundations/Sampling-and-representativeness.lingua#r_0F0VEC42GPQPRZM0S7MXF7GAEH") { r_3FKHJDJF9DDFFMVTVQJN7PAX34
**Read:** [Geological Structures: a Practical Introduction — Waldron and Snyder](../sources/Geological-Structures-Waldron-Snyder.lingua#r_2RC4KMK6ADFX90708NZH2F6RRW), the field-mapping and orientation record guidance; [Levantamento da geodiversidade — SGB technical guide, volume 5 version 4](../sources/SGB-Geodiversity-Methods.lingua#r_7QW5KYAYK9SB2MT3R6MNCFAQWK), observation/database/product relationships; [GeMS — Geologic Map Schema — USGS](../sources/GeMS-Map-Schema.lingua#r_6JP32YH79HZAV8JYYKVV1WER0J), the observation, orientation, references, and confidence concepts.

Assign each observation and specimen an identity and preserve location/reference, date, observer, method, scale/orientation, description, context, and uncertainty. A photograph needs a connection to the actual feature and field note; a specimen needs its source, orientation where relevant, and handling history. An isolated rock name cannot reconstruct that evidence.

Separate original observations, interpreted units, corrected measurements, working hypotheses, and later laboratory results. Preserve relationships across maps, sections, images, specimens, analyses, and revisions. A revised interpretation should explain what changed without silently replacing the original description or losing a link between specimen and location.

Use ordinary tables, sketches, and Records at the scale of the work. A structured format can help transfer information, but its fields do not guarantee that a critical observation was made or interpreted correctly. Identify gaps explicitly and keep confidence/locational quality distinct from a categorical material name.

**Work:** produce a small original field-record set with linked sketches, observations, and hypothetical or authorized specimen descriptions. Have another reader trace a mapped conclusion back to the original record. Introduce and correct one identity or orientation error, preserving the reason for the correction.

Follow [A specimen, a sampling target, and a result about a population](../../../../hephaestus/inquiry/metrology/foundations/Sampling-and-representativeness.lingua#r_0F0VEC42GPQPRZM0S7MXF7GAEH) for the shared distinction between a specimen, a sampling target, and a population result. Its geology continuation returns to a concrete sampling design. Reuse that general measurement reasoning while retaining the unique spatial and historical context of geological evidence.

